## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The recorded evidence shows that in the third quarter of 2020, the United States real GDP grew at an annualized rate of 33.1% (7.4% on a non-annualized basis), which is indeed the highest quarterly growth rate in U.S. history since official record-keeping began in 1947. The previous record from the first quarter of 1950 was 3.9% non-annualized (approximately 16.5% annualized), which is slightly less than half of the 2020 rate, making the 2020 rate about double—not "almost triple"—the previous record. Crucially, this record-breaking growth was a rebound that immediately followed a historic contraction of 32.9% annualized (9.5% non-annualized) in the second quarter of 2020 due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Despite the Q3 surge, the overall U.S. GDP level remained 3.5% lower than its pre-pandemic level in the fourth quarter of 2019.

Since the retrieved evidence is highly detailed and clear, no further investigation is required. I will proceed directly to Step 3.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the evidence, the claim is classified as `cherry-picking`. While the 33.1% annualized GDP growth rate is a real, record-breaking figure, presenting it in isolation to suggest the economy was performing better than ever is highly misleading. This historic growth was a mathematical rebound from an equally historic contraction of 32.9% in the preceding quarter, and the overall economy remained smaller than its pre-pandemic level. Furthermore, while the previous record from the 1950s was indeed less than half of this figure, the claim's assertion that the new rate is "almost triple" the previous record is mathematically incorrect, as it is actually about double. Therefore, omitting the context of the prior quarter's collapse and the net economic standing creates a significantly distorted impression of the country's economic health.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is classified as cherry-picking because, while the United States real GDP did grow at a record-breaking annualized rate of 33.1% (7.4% non-annualized) in the third quarter of 2020 as reported by the [Bureau of Economic Analysis](https://trumpwhitehouse.archives.gov/articles/great-american-recovery-third-quarter-gdp-blows-past-expectations/), presenting this figure in isolation is highly misleading. According to [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/29/economy/gdp-report-third-quarter/index.html), this was indeed the highest quarterly growth rate since 1947, but it was a mathematical rebound that immediately followed a historic contraction of 32.9% annualized in the second quarter of 2020, as documented by the [Peter G. Peterson Foundation](https://www.pgpf.org/blog/2023/04/real-gdp-falls-a-record-breaking-329-in-second-quarter). Furthermore, [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/gdp-q3-growth-33-1-percent/) reported that the overall GDP level remained 3.5% lower than its pre-pandemic level in late 2019. Finally, data from [USAFacts](https://usafacts.org/articles/gdp-grew-74-third-quarter-2020-remains-lower-it-was-last-year/) shows that the previous record from 1950 (3.9% non-annualized) was slightly less than half of the 2020 rate, meaning the new rate was roughly double, not "almost triple," the previous record.